Title = {Neonicotinoid insecticides and male reproductive health: A comprehensive
   assessment of sperm DNA integrity and semen quality in human seminal
   plasma and serum},
Journal = {JOURNAL OF HAZARDOUS MATERIALS ADVANCES},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {22},
Month = {MAY},
Abstract = {Background Given the widespread global use of neonicotinoid insecticides
   (NEOs) and growing concerns regarding their potential human health
   impacts, the association between NEO exposure and male reproductive
   health remains an area requiring in-depth investigation. This study
   aimed to comprehensively assess the exposure levels of NEOs and their
   metabolites in human seminal plasma and serum, and to explore their
   relationships with various semen quality parameters, particularly sperm
   DNA integrity. Methods We enrolled 419 men from a reproductive medicine
   center and collected paired seminal plasma and serum samples. Using a
   liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS), 15 NEOs and
   their metabolites were quantitatively analyzed. Comprehensive semen
   quality data, including conventional semen analysis and advanced sperm
   DNA integrity assessments (DNA fragmentation index {[}DFI] and high DNA
   stainability {[}HDS]), were obtained. Subsequently, generalized linear
   models (GLM), weighted quantile sum (WQS) regression, and Bayesian
   kernel machine regression (BKMR) were employed to analyze the
   associations between NEO exposure and semen quality parameters. Results
   This study revealed detectable NEO residues in all analyzed samples,
   with N-dm-ACE being the most prevalent NEO metabolite in both seminal
   plasma and serum, at significantly higher concentrations than its parent
   compound. Our findings demonstrated negative associations between NEO
   exposure in seminal plasma and overall semen quality, particularly
   detrimental effects on sperm concentration and DNA integrity. We found
   that men in the highest tertile of seminal plasma N-dm-ACE exposure
   exhibited significantly elevated sperm DFI (P-trend < 0.05). WQS and
   BKMR models further confirmed N-dm-ACE as a key contributor within the
   NEO mixture to increased DFI and HDS. Conclusion This cross-sectional
   study provides evidence suggesting that human exposure to NEOs,
   particularly N-dm-ACE, is significantly associated with altered male
   semen quality parameters, characterized by increased DNA fragmentation.
   These findings underscore the pervasive nature of NEO exposure and its
   potential adverse effects on male reproductive health. Our results
   suggest that the potential impacts of these environmental contaminants
   should be thoroughly considered in male fertility risk assessment and
   public health policy development.},
